Flamenco on Arch Linux
======================

Getting started
---------------

These packages provide systemd template services. The instance name is the
Unix user that runs Flamenco:

  sudo systemctl enable --now flamenco-manager@myuser.service
  sudo systemctl enable --now flamenco-worker@myuser.service

Manager stores its configuration and state in:

  /var/lib/flamenco-manager-myuser

Worker uses:

  /var/lib/flamenco-worker-myuser

systemd creates these directories with the correct ownership. The services do
not need a logged-in desktop session and are not enabled automatically.

Configuration
-------------

Manager reads flamenco-manager.yaml from its directory, and Worker reads
flamenco-worker.yaml from its directory. For example, a Worker can connect to
a Manager with:

  manager_url: http://manager-host:8080/

Manager autodiscovery can be used instead. Restarting a Worker through
Flamenco is already supported by the packaged service; there is no need to add
restart_exit_code to the YAML file.

The Worker service sets FLAMENCO_HOME to its local /var/lib directory. This is
where Worker credentials and its task-update database live. Keep that directory
local and unique to each Worker. Shared project and render storage is configured
separately in Flamenco Manager.

Network storage
---------------

Declare network filesystems in /etc/fstab and include _netdev. For example:

  server:/export /mnt/render nfs4 _netdev 0 0

The packaged services start after network-online.target and remote-fs.target.
If a service must not start unless a particular path is mounted, add a systemd
drop-in:

  sudo systemctl edit flamenco-worker@myuser.service

  [Unit]
  RequiresMountsFor=/mnt/render

There is normally no reason to change Flamenco's working directory or
FLAMENCO_HOME just to use shared render storage.

